# event_loop.bot -- the "std::event_loop" namespace: one thread-local
# scheduler of ordinary Botlish coroutines and native event sources
# (EVENT-LOOP-MILESTONE-1.md; the evidence it promotes is
# EVENT-LOOP-EXPLORATION.md).
#
#   with context std::event_loop::create(64)          the scheduler (Loop)
#   with context DRIVER                               any context implementing
#                                                     std::event_loop::Driver
#   coroutine {step, first} = task(...)               an ordinary coroutine
#   std::event_loop::adopt(step, first)               ... handed to the loop
#   std::event_loop::run()                            turns until nothing is live
#
# Three responsibilities, kept apart:
#
# * The SCHEDULER (this module, ordinary Botlish over MutableVectors,
#   MutableArrays and the existing coroutines) owns tasks, ready queues,
#   registrations, timers, the scheduling turn, fairness, revocation and
#   shutdown. It knows no operating system: no descriptor, handle or clock
#   source appears here.
# * The DRIVER is a context implementing the context trait Driver below: it
#   supplies time, waiting, wakeups and native records, and nothing of the
#   scheduling rules. Only the entry program installs contexts, so only the
#   application chooses the driver; two installed drivers are
#   AMBIGUOUS-CONTEXT-IMPLEMENTATION and none MISSING-CONTEXT -- importing a
#   library never changes who drives the loop. The deterministic driver is
#   std::event_loop::deterministic (every backend); the production Linux
#   driver is the package packages/event_loop_linux.
# * EVENT SOURCES register with the loop (source, channel, timer, observer)
#   and deliver records tagged with a registration's identity. A library that
#   requires only Loop can register, post, wait and revoke; it cannot make the
#   loop wait or turn, or drop its tasks -- run, turn, sync, shutdown and
#   abandon also require the Driver, which is the application's.
#
# Tasks are ordinary coroutines of one protocol,
#
#     Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}
#
# built (eagerly: the first segment runs inside the spawner) by whoever
# spawns them and handed over with adopt, which moves the handle into the
# Loop. Adopting twice, or resuming after adopting, is USE-AFTER-MOVE. A
# task's segment ends by yielding (yield_now, wait, sleep) or by returning;
# its children are ordinary coroutines it resumes directly. Nothing is
# allocated per dispatch.
#
# THE SCHEDULING TURN, in order:
#
#   1. wait     the driver blocks until a native record, a wakeup or the
#               earliest deadline a task waits on -- or polls, when ready
#               work exists. A timer nobody waits on imposes no deadline.
#   2. collect  holds the driver may release (backpressure: below), expired
#               timers (deadline order, registration order among equal
#               deadlines), then at most `budget` native records, which the
#               driver hands over round-robin among its sources. Each record
#               goes to its registration (its generation checked): the first
#               waiter becomes ready, else the event is queued under the
#               registration's Delivery policy.
#   3. dispatch exactly the tasks ready when this phase starts, each once,
#               in the order they became ready (FIFO, whatever made them
#               ready). Work made ready during the phase -- a yield, a post,
#               an adoption, a revocation's wakes -- runs in the next turn.
#   4. observe  every observer registration live when the phase starts runs
#               its waiting task once, in registration order -- only in a
#               turn whose collect or dispatch did something. An empty turn
#               (a wakeup for nothing) runs no observer.
#
# FAIRNESS is cooperative. A ready task runs within one turn of becoming
# ready however many others yield or replenish the queue; a turn collects at
# most `budget` records; a driver serves its sources round-robin, so a
# flooding source cannot hold back a quiet one. A segment that never yields
# holds the thread: there is no preemption and no wall-clock bound.
#
# DELIVERY. A source or channel queues at most CAPACITY undelivered events
# and its Delivery policy says what happens beyond: Lossy counts and drops
# the excess (a stream where the latest matters); Coalesce keeps one pending
# event, the latest, with a count of the events it stands for (readiness,
# motion); Reliable never drops: a record the registration has no room for
# stays with the driver (hold_record), which takes nothing more from that
# source until the registration's queue has drained to half its capacity
# (release_held) -- the backpressure reaches the native producer -- and a
# post from Botlish code is Refused. Timers coalesce (missed intervals are
# counted, never queued).
#
# LIVENESS. run returns when no task is ready and no task waits on a
# registration that keeps the loop alive (timers, and sources registered
# :keep_alive), on stop, or when the driver reports it can never deliver
# again. Observers and channels never keep the loop alive.
#
# REVOCATION. revoke(reg) bumps the registration's generation: no handler
# invocation after it -- its queued events are discarded, a wake already
# queued for a task is turned into Wake::Revoked when the task is dispatched,
# records still in flight with the old generation are dropped as stale (also
# after the slot is reused), and its waiters wake with Wake::Revoked in the
# order they waited. Code already running is not undone; tasks a
# registration woke or started keep their own lifetimes; revoking twice, or
# through a stale copy, does nothing. A Reg is an identity, not an owner;
# shutdown revokes every registration.
#
# OWNERSHIP (LEXICAL-RESOURCE-CLEANUP-EXPLORATION-MILESTONE-1.md). A
# registration made by owned_source, owned_channel, owned_timer, owned_after,
# owned_every or owned_observer has an Owner: a resource, affine, whose
# cleanup (`release`) revokes it. The owner holds the registration live
# until its lexical scope ends -- after its last use or not -- or until it is
# released explicitly (`release(owner)`); moving it (into a function, a
# result, a struct, a coroutine, a context's collection) moves that
# obligation, and a coroutine that holds it keeps it across suspension and
# releases it when it completes or is abandoned. Its Reg -- returned beside
# it, an ordinary identity -- waits, posts and observes as any Reg does, but
# cannot revoke: only the owner can (revoke of an owned registration does
# nothing and answers false).
#
# ERRORS. A task's declared failure (TaskFailed, its own IndexNotFound, or a
# DriverFault from a driver operation it made) ends that task, is recorded
# (take_outcomes) and affects nothing else. A
# failure in a task's first segment reaches its spawner (construction is
# eager). SchedulerFault is raised only when the scheduler's own state
# breaks an invariant -- a bug, never an application condition -- and ends
# run; DriverFault is the driver's report of a native failure. run or turn
# from inside a dispatch is LoopReentered.
import std::coroutine
import std::int
import std::mutable_array
import std::mutable_vector
import std::str

# A task's own failure, carried out of its coroutine to the scheduler.
error TaskFailed:
    code: int

# run or turn called while a task is being dispatched (a nested loop).
error LoopReentered

# The scheduler's own state broke an invariant (an index it derived from its
# own tables was out of range): a bug in the scheduler, never an application
# error. run and turn fail with it, and a task that receives it from a
# scheduler operation should let it propagate.
error SchedulerFault

# The driver reports a native failure it cannot recover from (CODE: the
# driver's, e.g. a negated errno).
error DriverFault:
    code: int

# What made a waiting task ready.
enum Wake:
    Ready,
    Event,
    Timer,
    Turn,
    Revoked,
    Empty,

# What a source or channel does with an event it has no room for.
enum Delivery:
    Lossy,
    Coalesce,
    Reliable,

# What post did with an event.
enum Posted:
    Accepted,
    Coalesced,
    Dropped,
    Refused,
    NotLive,

# How a task ended (take_outcomes).
enum Ending:
    Returned,
    Failed,
    IndexNotFound,
    SchedulerFault,
    DriverFault,

# A registration's identity: its slot and the generation it had when made.
# Opaque: only this module makes one, so holding a Reg is the authority to
# post to, wait on and revoke that registration (an owned one's revocation is
# its Owner's).
opaque struct Reg:
    slot: int
    gen: int

# The owner of an owned registration (owned_source, owned_channel, ...): a
# resource (LEXICAL-RESOURCE-CLEANUP-EXPLORATION-MILESTONE-1.md). Its cleanup,
# `release`, revokes the registration where the owner's lexical scope ends,
# or where it is called explicitly; moving the owner moves that obligation.
opaque resource struct Owner:
    slot: int
    gen: int

# An owned registration: its owner, and its identity (a copy of which may go
# anywhere: once the owner revoked the registration, it is stale).
struct Owned:
    owner: Owner
    reg: Reg

# What woke a task. A: the event's first value (a timer: the intervals it
# missed; an observer: the turn), B: its second, COUNT: the native events the
# wake stands for (a coalesced event's merged count; a timer's expiries, 1 +
# missed; 0 for Ready, Revoked and Empty).
struct Event:
    reason: Wake
    a: int
    b: int
    count: int

# A finished task: its id (adopt's), how it ended, and its value (the
# return value; TaskFailed's or DriverFault's code; 0 otherwise).
struct Outcome:
    task: int
    ending: Ending
    value: int

# The loop's counters (stats).
struct Stats:
    turns: int
    idle_turns: int
    dispatched: int
    observed: int
    collected: int
    timers_fired: int
    stale_records: int
    invalid_records: int
    suppressed_wakes: int
    dropped_events: int
    coalesced_events: int
    refused_posts: int
    held_records: int
    released_holds: int
    spawned: int
    completed: int
    failed: int
    reentered: int
    max_ready: int
    ready: int
    parked: int
    timers_waiting: int
    registrations: int
    outcomes_dropped: int

# The environment the loop waits in (a driver). Times are microseconds of one
# monotonic clock.
#
# * clock() reads it.
# * block(DEADLINE) returns when a record is available, a wakeup arrives or
#   DEADLINE passes (-1: no deadline; a deadline not after now -- 0 is always
#   one -- means poll: do not wait), with the number of records available,
#   or -1 when no record can ever arrive again (a script that ran out).
# * take_record() makes the next available record current and returns its
#   registration slot (-1: none). Records come round-robin over the
#   driver's sources (one record per source in turn; one source's records in
#   order); a held source yields nothing until released. record_gen,
#   record_a and record_b read the current record.
# * hold_record() keeps the current record, unconsumed, first in its source
#   and takes nothing more from that source until release_held(SLOT) is
#   called for the record's slot (Delivery::Reliable's backpressure).
#
# A driver adds no scheduling rule: turns, ordering, revocation and
# ownership are the scheduler's under every driver.
context trait Driver:
    fn clock() -> int errors DriverFault
    fn block(deadline: int) -> int errors DriverFault
    fn take_record() -> int errors DriverFault
    fn record_gen() -> int errors DriverFault
    fn record_a() -> int errors DriverFault
    fn record_b() -> int errors DriverFault
    fn hold_record() -> int errors DriverFault
    fn release_held(slot: int) -> int errors DriverFault

# A generation is carried by native records as a C int: a slot whose
# generation reaches this bound is retired, never reused, so a record's
# generation can never be mistaken for a later one.
private max_generation = 1073741824

# Registration kinds.
private kind_free = 0
private kind_timer = 1
private kind_source = 2
private kind_channel = 3
private kind_turn = 4

# Wake reason codes (the task table); Wake at the interface.
private w_ready = 0
private w_event = 1
private w_timer = 2
private w_turn = 3
private w_revoked = 4
private w_empty = 5

# Delivery codes.
private d_lossy = 0
private d_coalesce = 1
private d_reliable = 2

# What deliver did (internal).
private dv_stale = 0
private dv_woke = 1
private dv_queued = 2
private dv_coalesced = 3
private dv_dropped = 4
private dv_full = 5
private dv_invalid = 6

# Registration rows (stride rs).
private rs = 21
private r_gen = 0
private r_kind = 1
private r_live = 2
private r_keep = 3
private r_whead = 4
private r_wtail = 5
private r_nwait = 6
private r_deadline = 7
private r_period = 8
private r_cap = 9
private r_phead = 10
private r_ptail = 11
private r_npend = 12
private r_dropped = 13
private r_seq = 14
private r_delivered = 15
private r_policy = 16
private r_held = 17
private r_hpos = 18
private r_inflight = 19
private r_owned = 20

# Task rows (stride ts).
private ts = 10
private t_pos = 0
private t_next = 1
private t_reason = 2
private t_slot = 3
private t_gen = 4
private t_a = 5
private t_b = 6
private t_count = 7
private t_live = 8
private t_serial = 9

# Pending event nodes (stride es): a, b, count, next.
private es = 4

# Outcome records (stride os): task, ending code, value.
private os = 3

# Registers (st).
private s_turn = 0
private s_now = 1
private s_dispatching = 2
private s_stopping = 3
private s_budget = 4
private s_seq = 5
private s_tracing = 6
private s_dispatched = 7
private s_collected = 8
private s_stale = 9
private s_failed = 10
private s_completed = 11
private s_wake_reason = 12
private s_wake_slot = 13
private s_wake_a = 14
private s_wake_b = 15
private s_wake_count = 16
private s_ntasks = 17
private s_nregs = 18
private s_nevents = 19
private s_keepwait = 20
private s_suppressed = 21
private s_reentered = 22
private s_observed = 23
private s_spawned = 24
private s_maxready = 25
private s_turn_dispatched = 26
private s_current = 27
private s_idle_turns = 28
private s_faulted = 29
private s_invalid = 30
private s_dropped = 31
private s_coalesced = 32
private s_refused = 33
private s_held = 34
private s_released = 35
private s_fired = 36
private s_outcome_cap = 37
private s_outcomes_dropped = 38
private s_live_regs = 39
# scratch registers of the per-turn loops (no allocation per turn)
private s_tmp_fired = 40
private s_tmp_count = 41
private s_tmp_taken = 42
private s_tmp_i = 43
private s_tmp_obs = 44
private s_tmp_dead = 45
# what try_take took (read by try_take itself)
private s_take_a = 46
private s_take_b = 47
private s_take_count = 48
private s_count = 49

opaque context struct Loop:
    ready_in: MutableVector[Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}]
    ready_in_id: MutableVector[int]
    ready_out: MutableVector[Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}]
    ready_out_id: MutableVector[int]
    parked: MutableVector[Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}]
    parked_id: MutableVector[int]
    tasks: MutableVector[int]
    t_free: MutableVector[int]
    regs: MutableVector[int]
    r_free: MutableVector[int]
    events: MutableVector[int]
    e_free: MutableVector[int]
    heap: MutableVector[int]
    observers: MutableVector[int]
    observer_gens: MutableVector[int]
    releases: MutableVector[int]
    outcomes: MutableVector[int]
    st: MutableArray[int]
    trace: MutableVector[str]

private fn no_tasks() -> MutableVector[Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}]:
    std::mutable_vector::from_list([])

private fn no_ints() -> MutableVector[int]:
    std::mutable_vector::from_list([])

private fn no_strs() -> MutableVector[str]:
    std::mutable_vector::from_list([])

private nomethod fn registers(budget: int, tracing: int) -> MutableArray[int]:
    initial = loop i from 0 to s_count:
        if i == s_budget:
            budget
        elif i == s_tracing:
            tracing
        elif i == s_outcome_cap:
            4096
        else:
            0
    std::mutable_array::from_list(initial)

# A Loop that collects at most BUDGET native records per turn (at least 1).
# :trace records every scheduling decision in the trace (tests: trace).
fn create(budget: int, flags :trace) -> Loop:
    tracing = if trace:
        1
    else:
        0
    least = if budget < 1:
        1
    else:
        budget
    Loop {
        ready_in: no_tasks(), ready_in_id: no_ints(),
        ready_out: no_tasks(), ready_out_id: no_ints(),
        parked: no_tasks(), parked_id: no_ints(),
        tasks: no_ints(), t_free: no_ints(),
        regs: no_ints(), r_free: no_ints(),
        events: no_ints(), e_free: no_ints(),
        heap: no_ints(),
        observers: no_ints(), observer_gens: no_ints(),
        releases: no_ints(), outcomes: no_ints(),
        st: registers(least, tracing), trace: no_strs()
    }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Registers, rows, tracing

private nomethod fn set_reg(i: int, v: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    ev.st.set(i, v)

private nomethod fn bump(i: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    ev.st.set(i, ev.st.at(i) + 1)

private nomethod fn rget(slot: int, f: int, context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    ev.regs.at(slot * rs + f)

private nomethod fn rset(slot: int, f: int, v: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    ev.regs.swap(slot * rs + f, v)

private nomethod fn tget(id: int, f: int, context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    ev.tasks.at(id * ts + f)

private nomethod fn tset(id: int, f: int, v: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    ev.tasks.swap(id * ts + f, v)

private fn tracing_on?(context ev: Loop) -> bool errors IndexNotFound:
    ev.st.at(s_tracing) == 1

# TEXT followed by N's decimal text.
private nomethod fn cat(text: str, n: int) -> str:
    text.concat(std::int::to_string(n))

private fn note(text: str, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    ev.trace.push(cat("t=", ev.st.at(s_turn)).concat(": ").concat(text))

# Whether the loop records its scheduling decisions (create's :trace).
fn tracing?(context ev: Loop) -> bool errors SchedulerFault:
    tracing_on?():
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# The trace so far, oldest first: the scheduler's decisions (with :trace)
# and the application's own log lines, each "t=TURN: TEXT".
fn trace(context ev: Loop) -> List[str] errors SchedulerFault:
    lines = loop i from 0 to ev.trace.length():
        ev.trace.at(i):
            on IndexNotFound:
                fail SchedulerFault
    lines

# An application line in the trace (recorded also without :trace).
fn log(text: str, context ev: Loop) errors SchedulerFault:
    note(text):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

private fn reg_of(i: int, context ev: Loop) -> int errors SchedulerFault:
    ev.st.at(i):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# The loop's time: the driver's clock as read at the start of the current
# turn (or by sync). after, every and timers count from it.
fn now(context ev: Loop) -> int errors SchedulerFault:
    reg_of(s_now)

# The number of the current turn (0 before the first).
fn turn_number(context ev: Loop) -> int errors SchedulerFault:
    reg_of(s_turn)

# The id of the task being dispatched (-1 outside a dispatch).
fn current_task(context ev: Loop) -> int errors SchedulerFault:
    if reg_of(s_dispatching) == 1:
        reg_of(s_current)
    else:
        -1

private fn ready_count(context ev: Loop) -> int:
    ev.ready_in.length() + ev.ready_out.length()

private fn stats_checked(context ev: Loop) -> Stats errors IndexNotFound:
    Stats {
        turns: ev.st.at(s_turn), idle_turns: ev.st.at(s_idle_turns),
        dispatched: ev.st.at(s_dispatched), observed: ev.st.at(s_observed),
        collected: ev.st.at(s_collected), timers_fired: ev.st.at(s_fired),
        stale_records: ev.st.at(s_stale), invalid_records: ev.st.at(s_invalid),
        suppressed_wakes: ev.st.at(s_suppressed), dropped_events: ev.st.at(s_dropped),
        coalesced_events: ev.st.at(s_coalesced), refused_posts: ev.st.at(s_refused),
        held_records: ev.st.at(s_held), released_holds: ev.st.at(s_released),
        spawned: ev.st.at(s_spawned), completed: ev.st.at(s_completed),
        failed: ev.st.at(s_failed), reentered: ev.st.at(s_reentered),
        max_ready: ev.st.at(s_maxready), ready: ready_count(),
        parked: ev.parked.length(), timers_waiting: ev.heap.length(),
        registrations: ev.st.at(s_live_regs), outcomes_dropped: ev.st.at(s_outcomes_dropped)
    }

# A snapshot of the loop's counters.
fn stats(context ev: Loop) -> Stats errors SchedulerFault:
    stats_checked():
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Outcomes

private nomethod fn record_outcome(id: int, ending: int, value: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    if ev.outcomes.length() >= ev.st.at(s_outcome_cap) * os:
        bump(s_outcomes_dropped)
        return unit
    ev.outcomes.push(id)
    ev.outcomes.push(ending)
    ev.outcomes.push(value)

private fn ending_of(code: int) -> Ending:
    if code == 0:
        Ending::Returned
    elif code == 1:
        Ending::Failed
    elif code == 2:
        Ending::IndexNotFound
    elif code == 3:
        Ending::SchedulerFault
    else:
        Ending::DriverFault

private fn outcomes_checked(context ev: Loop) -> List[Outcome] errors IndexNotFound:
    n = ev.outcomes.length()
    out = loop i from 0 to n:
        if i.mod(os) != 0:
            continue
        Outcome {task: ev.outcomes.at(i), ending: ending_of(ev.outcomes.at(i + 1)), value: ev.outcomes.at(i + 2)}
    ev.outcomes.clear()
    out

# The tasks that ended since the last call, in the order they ended (at
# most 4096 are kept between calls; the excess is counted in
# Stats.outcomes_dropped).
fn take_outcomes(context ev: Loop) -> List[Outcome] errors SchedulerFault:
    outcomes_checked():
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tasks

private fn new_task(context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    id = if ev.t_free.empty?():
        n = ev.st.at(s_ntasks)
        loop f from 0 to ts:
            ev.tasks.push(0)
        set_reg(s_ntasks, n + 1)
        n
    else:
        ev.t_free.pop()
    tset(id, t_pos, -1)
    tset(id, t_next, -1)
    tset(id, t_reason, w_ready)
    tset(id, t_slot, -1)
    tset(id, t_gen, 0)
    tset(id, t_a, 0)
    tset(id, t_b, 0)
    tset(id, t_count, 0)
    tset(id, t_live, 1)
    tset(id, t_serial, ev.st.at(s_spawned))
    bump(s_spawned)
    id

private nomethod fn retire_task(id: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    tset(id, t_live, 0)
    ev.t_free.push(id)

# Make task ID (handle STEP) ready with REASON from registration SLOT/GEN.
private nomethod fn push_ready(step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}, id: int, reason: int, slot: int, gen: int, a: int, b: int, count: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    tset(id, t_reason, reason)
    tset(id, t_slot, slot)
    tset(id, t_gen, gen)
    tset(id, t_a, a)
    tset(id, t_b, b)
    tset(id, t_count, count)
    ev.ready_in.push(step)
    ev.ready_in_id.push(id)
    n = ready_count()
    if n > ev.st.at(s_maxready):
        set_reg(s_maxready, n)

private fn refill(context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    loop:
        if ev.ready_in.empty?():
            break
        ev.ready_out.push(ev.ready_in.pop())
        ev.ready_out_id.push(ev.ready_in_id.pop())

# The id of the task that has been ready longest, which the next pop_ready
# returns (call before it).
private fn front_ready(context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    if ev.ready_out.empty?():
        refill()
    ev.ready_out_id.at(ev.ready_out_id.length() - 1)

private fn pop_ready(context ev: Loop) -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]} errors IndexNotFound:
    if ev.ready_out.empty?():
        refill()
    ev.ready_out_id.pop()
    ev.ready_out.pop()

private nomethod fn complete(id: int, value: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    bump(s_completed)
    record_outcome(tget(id, t_serial), 0, value)
    retire_task(id)

# Take the parked task ID out of the parked set (swap-remove) and return its
# handle.
private nomethod fn unpark(id: int, context ev: Loop) -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]} errors IndexNotFound:
    i = tget(id, t_pos)
    last = ev.parked.length() - 1
    tset(id, t_pos, -1)
    if i == last:
        ev.parked_id.pop()
        return ev.parked.pop()
    moved = ev.parked_id.pop()
    ev.parked_id.swap(i, moved)
    tset(moved, t_pos, i)
    moved_step = ev.parked.pop()
    ev.parked.swap(i, moved_step)

# The first waiter of SLOT, removed from its FIFO (-1 when none).
private nomethod fn pop_waiter(slot: int, context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    id = rget(slot, r_whead)
    if id == -1:
        return -1
    next = tget(id, t_next)
    rset(slot, r_whead, next)
    if next == -1:
        rset(slot, r_wtail, -1)
    tset(id, t_next, -1)
    rset(slot, r_nwait, rget(slot, r_nwait) - 1)
    if rget(slot, r_keep) == 1:
        set_reg(s_keepwait, ev.st.at(s_keepwait) - 1)
    id

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The deadline heap: a binary min-heap of the timers some task waits on,
# ordered by (deadline, registration sequence); each timer's row holds its
# position (r_hpos, -1 outside the heap). A timer enters it when its first
# waiter parks and leaves it when its last waiter is woken or it is
# revoked, so a timer nobody waits on imposes no deadline and costs nothing.

private nomethod fn earlier?(x: int, y: int, context ev: Loop) -> bool errors IndexNotFound:
    dx = rget(x, r_deadline)
    dy = rget(y, r_deadline)
    if dx != dy:
        dx < dy
    else:
        rget(x, r_seq) < rget(y, r_seq)

private nomethod fn heap_put(i: int, slot: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    ev.heap.swap(i, slot)
    rset(slot, r_hpos, i)

private nomethod fn sift_up(i: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    if i == 0:
        return unit
    parent = (i - 1).shift_right(1)
    me = ev.heap.at(i)
    them = ev.heap.at(parent)
    if earlier?(me, them):
        heap_put(parent, me)
        heap_put(i, them)
        sift_up(parent)

private nomethod fn sift_down(i: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    n = ev.heap.length()
    left = i * 2 + 1
    if left >= n:
        return unit
    right = left + 1
    child = if right < n and earlier?(ev.heap.at(right), ev.heap.at(left)):
        right
    else:
        left
    me = ev.heap.at(i)
    them = ev.heap.at(child)
    if earlier?(them, me):
        heap_put(i, them)
        heap_put(child, me)
        sift_down(child)

private nomethod fn heap_insert(slot: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    i = ev.heap.length()
    ev.heap.push(slot)
    rset(slot, r_hpos, i)
    sift_up(i)

private nomethod fn heap_remove(slot: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    i = rget(slot, r_hpos)
    if i < 0:
        return unit
    rset(slot, r_hpos, -1)
    last = ev.heap.pop()
    if last == slot:
        return unit
    heap_put(i, last)
    sift_down(i)
    sift_up(rget(last, r_hpos))

# The earliest deadline a task waits on (-1: none): the top of the heap.
private fn next_deadline(context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    if ev.heap.empty?():
        -1
    else:
        rget(ev.heap.at(0), r_deadline)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Registrations

private nomethod fn new_reg(kind: int, keep: int, policy: int, cap: int, context ev: Loop) -> Reg errors IndexNotFound:
    slot = if ev.r_free.empty?():
        n = ev.st.at(s_nregs)
        loop f from 0 to rs:
            ev.regs.push(0)
        set_reg(s_nregs, n + 1)
        rset(n, r_gen, 1)
        n
    else:
        ev.r_free.pop()
    rset(slot, r_kind, kind)
    rset(slot, r_live, 1)
    rset(slot, r_keep, keep)
    rset(slot, r_whead, -1)
    rset(slot, r_wtail, -1)
    rset(slot, r_nwait, 0)
    rset(slot, r_deadline, -1)
    rset(slot, r_period, 0)
    rset(slot, r_cap, cap)
    rset(slot, r_phead, -1)
    rset(slot, r_ptail, -1)
    rset(slot, r_npend, 0)
    rset(slot, r_dropped, 0)
    rset(slot, r_seq, ev.st.at(s_seq))
    rset(slot, r_delivered, 0)
    rset(slot, r_policy, policy)
    rset(slot, r_held, 0)
    rset(slot, r_hpos, -1)
    rset(slot, r_inflight, 0)
    rset(slot, r_owned, 0)
    bump(s_seq)
    bump(s_live_regs)
    Reg {slot: slot, gen: rget(slot, r_gen)}

# Whether SLOT names a row of the table.
private nomethod fn in_table?(slot: int, context ev: Loop) -> bool errors IndexNotFound:
    slot >= 0 and slot < ev.st.at(s_nregs)

private fn live_checked?(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> bool errors IndexNotFound:
    if not in_table?(reg.slot):
        return false
    rget(reg.slot, r_gen) == reg.gen and rget(reg.slot, r_live) == 1

# Whether REG still names a live registration.
fn live?(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> bool errors SchedulerFault:
    live_checked?(reg):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# The tag a native producer puts on its records for REG: the slot and the
# generation (each fits a C int). Nothing can be done with them but
# delivering records: a Reg cannot be made from them.
fn native_slot(reg: Reg) -> int:
    reg.slot

fn native_generation(reg: Reg) -> int:
    reg.gen

private fn delivery_code(delivery: Delivery) -> int:
    if delivery == Delivery::Lossy:
        d_lossy
    elif delivery == Delivery::Coalesce:
        d_coalesce
    else:
        d_reliable

private fn least_one(n: int) -> int:
    if n < 1:
        1
    else:
        n

private nomethod fn trace_reg(what: str, reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    if tracing_on?():
        note(cat(what, reg.slot).concat(cat(" gen ", reg.gen)))

private nomethod fn timer_checked(deadline: int, period: int, context ev: Loop) -> Reg errors IndexNotFound:
    reg = new_reg(kind_timer, 1, d_coalesce, 1)
    rset(reg.slot, r_deadline, deadline)
    rset(reg.slot, r_period, period)
    if tracing_on?():
        note(cat("timer ", reg.slot).concat(cat(" at ", deadline)).concat(cat(" every ", period)))
    reg

# A one-shot timer at DEADLINE (loop time), or a repeating one (PERIOD > 0)
# whose first expiry is DEADLINE. A timer keeps the loop alive while a task
# waits on it. A repeating timer never queues missed expiries: one wake
# reports how many intervals were missed (Event.a) and its next deadline
# stays on the period's grid. A one-shot timer wakes every task waiting on
# it when it expires, and is then spent (no longer live).
nomethod fn timer(deadline: int, period: int, context ev: Loop) -> Reg errors SchedulerFault:
    timer_checked(deadline, period):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# A one-shot timer DELAY microseconds after the loop's time.
fn after(delay: int, context ev: Loop) -> Reg errors SchedulerFault:
    timer(reg_of(s_now) + delay, 0)

# A repeating timer of PERIOD microseconds (> 0), first PERIOD after the
# loop's time.
fn every(period: int, context ev: Loop) -> Reg errors SchedulerFault:
    timer(reg_of(s_now) + period, least_one(period))

private nomethod fn source_checked(keep: int, policy: int, cap: int, context ev: Loop) -> Reg errors IndexNotFound:
    reg = new_reg(kind_source, keep, policy, cap)
    trace_reg("source ", reg)
    reg

# A source of native records (the driver delivers them; Botlish code may
# post too). DELIVERY says what happens when CAPACITY (at least 1)
# undelivered events are already queued. :keep_alive: a task waiting on it
# keeps the loop alive (a listening socket); without it, it does not (an
# optional input).
nomethod fn source(delivery: Delivery, capacity: int, flags :keep_alive, context ev: Loop) -> Reg errors SchedulerFault:
    keep = if keep_alive:
        1
    else:
        0
    source_checked(keep, delivery_code(delivery), least_one(capacity)):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

private nomethod fn channel_checked(policy: int, cap: int, context ev: Loop) -> Reg errors IndexNotFound:
    reg = new_reg(kind_channel, 0, policy, cap)
    trace_reg("channel ", reg)
    reg

# A channel Botlish code posts to. It never keeps the loop alive by itself:
# only a running task can post to it.
nomethod fn channel(delivery: Delivery, capacity: int, context ev: Loop) -> Reg errors SchedulerFault:
    channel_checked(delivery_code(delivery), least_one(capacity)):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

private fn observer_checked(context ev: Loop) -> Reg errors IndexNotFound:
    reg = new_reg(kind_turn, 0, d_coalesce, 1)
    ev.observers.push(reg.slot)
    ev.observer_gens.push(reg.gen)
    trace_reg("observer ", reg)
    reg

# A once-per-turn registration: a task waiting on it runs once in the
# observer phase of every turn that collected or dispatched something
# (Event.a: the turn). It takes effect at the first observer phase that
# starts after this call, never keeps the loop alive, and never makes the
# loop poll: an idle loop sleeps. It is not a timer and not a frame clock
# (frame work is a repeating timer).
fn observer(context ev: Loop) -> Reg errors SchedulerFault:
    observer_checked():
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# Return SLOT's row to the free list (bumping its generation), or retire it
# when its generation would leave a C int.
private nomethod fn free_reg(slot: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    if rget(slot, r_kind) == kind_free:
        return unit
    gen = rget(slot, r_gen) + 1
    rset(slot, r_gen, gen)
    rset(slot, r_live, 0)
    rset(slot, r_kind, kind_free)
    set_reg(s_live_regs, ev.st.at(s_live_regs) - 1)
    if gen < max_generation:
        ev.r_free.push(slot)

# Ask the driver to release SLOT's held records at the next collect.
private nomethod fn schedule_release(slot: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    if rget(slot, r_held) == 1:
        rset(slot, r_held, 0)
        ev.releases.push(slot)

# A held registration's consumer took an event: release its source once at
# most half its capacity is queued (a low-water mark, so a consumer that
# takes one event per turn does not cost a hold and a release per record).
private nomethod fn release_if_room(slot: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    if rget(slot, r_npend) <= rget(slot, r_cap).shift_right(1):
        schedule_release(slot)

private fn revoke_slot(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> bool errors IndexNotFound:
    stale = if not in_table?(reg.slot):
        true
    else:
        rget(reg.slot, r_gen) != reg.gen or rget(reg.slot, r_kind) == kind_free
    if stale:
        if tracing_on?():
            note(cat("revoke stale ", reg.slot))
        return false
    slot = reg.slot
    discarded = rget(slot, r_npend)
    rset(slot, r_live, 0)
    # The queued events.
    loop:
        node = rget(slot, r_phead)
        if node == -1:
            break
        rset(slot, r_phead, ev.events.at(node * es + 3))
        ev.e_free.push(node)
    rset(slot, r_ptail, -1)
    rset(slot, r_npend, 0)
    heap_remove(slot)
    schedule_release(slot)
    if tracing_on?():
        note(cat("revoke ", slot).concat(cat(" discarding ", discarded)))
    # The waiters, in the order they waited.
    loop:
        id = pop_waiter(slot)
        if id == -1:
            break
        push_ready(unpark(id), id, w_revoked, slot, reg.gen, 0, 0, 0)
    free_reg(slot)
    true

private fn revoke_checked(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> bool errors IndexNotFound:
    if in_table?(reg.slot) and rget(reg.slot, r_gen) == reg.gen and rget(reg.slot, r_owned) == 1:
        # An owned registration: its Owner revokes it, never an identity.
        if tracing_on?():
            note(cat("revoke refused, owned ", reg.slot))
        return false
    revoke_slot(reg)

# Revoke REG: no handler invocation after this one. Its queued events are
# discarded, a wake already queued for a task becomes Wake::Revoked when the
# task is dispatched, records still in flight are dropped as stale, and
# every waiter is made ready with Wake::Revoked, in the order they waited.
# Running code is not undone and no task is cancelled. True when REG was
# live (or a spent one-shot timer); revoking twice, or a stale copy, does
# nothing (false). An owned registration is revoked by its Owner only
# (release): revoking it through its Reg does nothing (false).
fn revoke(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> bool errors SchedulerFault:
    revoke_checked(reg):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# Owned registrations (LEXICAL-RESOURCE-CLEANUP-EXPLORATION-MILESTONE-1.md).

# REG, made just now, owned: its Owner and its identity.
private nomethod fn own(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> Owned errors IndexNotFound:
    rset(reg.slot, r_owned, 1)
    Owned {owner: Owner {slot: reg.slot, gen: reg.gen}, reg: reg}

# The owned registrations: as source, channel, timer, after, every and
# observer, each with an Owner whose scope delimits it (Ownership, above).

private nomethod fn owned_source_checked(keep: int, policy: int, cap: int, context ev: Loop) -> Owned errors IndexNotFound:
    own(source_checked(keep, policy, cap))

# source's registration, owned: an Owned (its Owner and its Reg), revoked
# where the Owner's scope ends (Ownership, above).
nomethod fn owned_source(delivery: Delivery, capacity: int, flags :keep_alive, context ev: Loop) -> Owned errors SchedulerFault:
    keep = if keep_alive:
        1
    else:
        0
    owned_source_checked(keep, delivery_code(delivery), least_one(capacity)):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

private nomethod fn owned_channel_checked(policy: int, cap: int, context ev: Loop) -> Owned errors IndexNotFound:
    own(channel_checked(policy, cap))

# channel's registration, owned (owned_source).
nomethod fn owned_channel(delivery: Delivery, capacity: int, context ev: Loop) -> Owned errors SchedulerFault:
    owned_channel_checked(delivery_code(delivery), least_one(capacity)):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

private nomethod fn owned_timer_checked(deadline: int, period: int, context ev: Loop) -> Owned errors IndexNotFound:
    own(timer_checked(deadline, period))

# timer's registration, owned (owned_source).
nomethod fn owned_timer(deadline: int, period: int, context ev: Loop) -> Owned errors SchedulerFault:
    owned_timer_checked(deadline, period):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# after's registration, owned (owned_source).
fn owned_after(delay: int, context ev: Loop) -> Owned errors SchedulerFault:
    owned_timer(reg_of(s_now) + delay, 0)

# every's registration, owned (owned_source).
fn owned_every(period: int, context ev: Loop) -> Owned errors SchedulerFault:
    owned_timer(reg_of(s_now) + period, least_one(period))

private fn owned_observer_checked(context ev: Loop) -> Owned errors IndexNotFound:
    own(observer_checked())

# observer's registration, owned (owned_source).
fn owned_observer(context ev: Loop) -> Owned errors SchedulerFault:
    owned_observer_checked():
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# Release OWNER: revoke its registration (revoke's guarantees), now -- the
# owner's cleanup, which runs where the owner's lexical scope ends; calling
# it is the explicit early release. Revoking a registration that is gone
# already (shutdown revoked it) does nothing. A cleanup fails with nothing: a
# scheduler fault on the way is recorded, and the next run, turn or shutdown
# fails with SchedulerFault.
cleanup fn release(owner: Owner, context ev: Loop):
    {slot, gen} = owner
    revoke_slot(Reg {slot: slot, gen: gen}):
        on IndexNotFound:
            set_reg(s_faulted, 1):
                on IndexNotFound:
                    unit
            false

# Queue event (A, B) of COUNT native events on registration SLOT.
private nomethod fn enqueue(slot: int, a: int, b: int, count: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    node = if ev.e_free.empty?():
        n = ev.st.at(s_nevents)
        loop f from 0 to es:
            ev.events.push(0)
        set_reg(s_nevents, n + 1)
        n
    else:
        ev.e_free.pop()
    ev.events.swap(node * es, a)
    ev.events.swap(node * es + 1, b)
    ev.events.swap(node * es + 2, count)
    ev.events.swap(node * es + 3, -1)
    tail = rget(slot, r_ptail)
    if tail == -1:
        rset(slot, r_phead, node)
    else:
        ev.events.swap(tail * es + 3, node)
    rset(slot, r_ptail, node)
    rset(slot, r_npend, rget(slot, r_npend) + 1)

# Deliver event (A, B) to registration SLOT, expected at generation GEN:
# its first waiter is made ready, else the event is queued under the
# registration's policy (what happened: a dv_* code).
private nomethod fn deliver(slot: int, gen: int, a: int, b: int, context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    stale = if not in_table?(slot):
        true
    else:
        rget(slot, r_gen) != gen or rget(slot, r_live) != 1
    if stale:
        bump(s_stale)
        if tracing_on?():
            note(cat("stale record for ", slot).concat(cat(" gen ", gen)))
        return dv_stale
    kind = rget(slot, r_kind)
    if kind != kind_source and kind != kind_channel:
        # A record for a timer or an observer: the producer broke its
        # integration contract (records go to sources and channels only).
        bump(s_invalid)
        if tracing_on?():
            note(cat("invalid record for ", slot))
        return dv_invalid
    id = pop_waiter(slot)
    if id != -1:
        rset(slot, r_delivered, rget(slot, r_delivered) + 1)
        push_ready(unpark(id), id, w_event, slot, gen, a, b, 1)
        return dv_woke
    npend = rget(slot, r_npend)
    policy = rget(slot, r_policy)
    if policy == d_coalesce and npend > 0:
        tail = rget(slot, r_ptail)
        ev.events.swap(tail * es, a)
        ev.events.swap(tail * es + 1, b)
        ev.events.swap(tail * es + 2, ev.events.at(tail * es + 2) + 1)
        rset(slot, r_delivered, rget(slot, r_delivered) + 1)
        bump(s_coalesced)
        return dv_coalesced
    if npend >= rget(slot, r_cap):
        if policy == d_reliable:
            return dv_full
        rset(slot, r_dropped, rget(slot, r_dropped) + 1)
        bump(s_dropped)
        if tracing_on?():
            note(cat("overflow ", slot))
        return dv_dropped
    rset(slot, r_delivered, rget(slot, r_delivered) + 1)
    enqueue(slot, a, b, 1)
    dv_queued

private fn posted_of(code: int) -> Posted:
    if code == dv_woke or code == dv_queued:
        Posted::Accepted
    elif code == dv_coalesced:
        Posted::Coalesced
    elif code == dv_dropped:
        Posted::Dropped
    elif code == dv_full:
        Posted::Refused
    else:
        Posted::NotLive

private nomethod fn post_checked(reg: Reg, a: int, b: int, context ev: Loop) -> Posted errors IndexNotFound:
    if not live_checked?(reg):
        return Posted::NotLive
    code = deliver(reg.slot, reg.gen, a, b)
    if code == dv_full:
        bump(s_refused)
    posted_of(code)

# Post (A, B) to the source or channel REG from Botlish code (a task or the
# top level). Accepted: a waiter was woken or the event queued; Coalesced:
# merged into the pending event; Dropped: a Lossy registration was full
# (counted); Refused: a Reliable one was full (try again after its consumer
# took an event); NotLive: REG is revoked, spent, or not a source or channel.
fn post(reg: Reg, a: int, b: int, context ev: Loop) -> Posted errors SchedulerFault:
    post_checked(reg, a, b):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

private nomethod fn reg_field_checked(reg: Reg, f: int, context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    if live_checked?(reg):
        rget(reg.slot, f)
    else:
        0

private nomethod fn reg_field(reg: Reg, f: int, context ev: Loop) -> int errors SchedulerFault:
    reg_field_checked(reg, f):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# Events of REG queued and undelivered (0 when REG is not live).
fn pending(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> int errors SchedulerFault:
    reg_field(reg, r_npend)

# Events REG accepted (woke a waiter, queued or coalesced) / dropped for want
# of room (Lossy), while live.
fn delivered(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> int errors SchedulerFault:
    reg_field(reg, r_delivered)

fn dropped(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> int errors SchedulerFault:
    reg_field(reg, r_dropped)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Timers

# How many whole periods past DEADLINE NOW is (>= 1 when NOW >= DEADLINE):
# 1 + floor((now - deadline) / period), without division and without one
# step per missed interval -- by doubling, then a binary search.
private nomethod fn doubling(h: int, period: int, span: int) -> int:
    if h * period > span:
        h
    else:
        doubling(h * 2, period, span)

private nomethod fn largest_multiple(lo: int, step: int, top: int, period: int, span: int) -> int:
    if step == 0:
        return lo
    up = lo + step
    next = if up < top and up * period <= span:
        up
    else:
        lo
    largest_multiple(next, step.shift_right(1), top, period, span)

private nomethod fn periods_due(deadline: int, period: int, now: int) -> int:
    if now < deadline:
        return 0
    span = now - deadline
    if span < period:
        return 1
    top = doubling(1, period, span)
    largest_multiple(0, top, top, period, span) + 1

# A one-shot timer that expired: no longer live, its slot kept until every
# wake it made is dispatched (or it is revoked), so a revocation in between
# still suppresses those wakes.
private nomethod fn spend_timer(slot: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    rset(slot, r_live, 0)
    rset(slot, r_deadline, -1)
    if rget(slot, r_inflight) == 0:
        free_reg(slot)

# Timer SLOT is due at NOW and a task waits on it: a repeating timer wakes
# its first waiter (Event.a: the intervals missed, coalesced) and moves to
# its next deadline on the period's grid; a one-shot timer wakes every
# waiter and is spent.
private nomethod fn fire_timer(slot: int, now: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    deadline = rget(slot, r_deadline)
    period = rget(slot, r_period)
    gen = rget(slot, r_gen)
    bump(s_fired)
    if period > 0:
        due = periods_due(deadline, period, now)
        if tracing_on?():
            note(cat("timer ", slot).concat(cat(" fires late ", now - deadline)).concat(cat(" missed ", due - 1)))
        rset(slot, r_deadline, deadline + due * period)
        id = pop_waiter(slot)
        push_ready(unpark(id), id, w_timer, slot, gen, due - 1, 0, due)
        if rget(slot, r_nwait) > 0:
            sift_down(rget(slot, r_hpos))
        else:
            heap_remove(slot)
        return unit
    if tracing_on?():
        note(cat("timer ", slot).concat(cat(" fires late ", now - deadline)).concat(" missed 0"))
    heap_remove(slot)
    loop:
        id = pop_waiter(slot)
        if id == -1:
            break
        rset(slot, r_inflight, rget(slot, r_inflight) + 1)
        push_ready(unpark(id), id, w_timer, slot, gen, 0, 0, 1)
    spend_timer(slot)

# Expire the timers due at NOW, earliest deadline first (registration order
# among equal deadlines); returns how many fired. O(log n) per expiry.
private nomethod fn expire_timers(now: int, context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    set_reg(s_tmp_fired, 0)
    loop:
        if ev.heap.empty?():
            break
        slot = ev.heap.at(0)
        if rget(slot, r_deadline) > now:
            break
        fire_timer(slot, now)
        bump(s_tmp_fired)
    ev.st.at(s_tmp_fired)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Parking and adoption

# Take the first queued event of SLOT (which has one) for task ID.
private nomethod fn take_queued(step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}, id: int, slot: int, gen: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    node = rget(slot, r_phead)
    a = ev.events.at(node * es)
    b = ev.events.at(node * es + 1)
    count = ev.events.at(node * es + 2)
    next = ev.events.at(node * es + 3)
    rset(slot, r_phead, next)
    if next == -1:
        rset(slot, r_ptail, -1)
    rset(slot, r_npend, rget(slot, r_npend) - 1)
    ev.e_free.push(node)
    release_if_room(slot)
    push_ready(step, id, w_event, slot, gen, a, b, count)

# Park task ID on registration SLOT, or make it ready at once when the
# registration has something for it (or is not live).
private nomethod fn park(step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}, id: int, slot: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    if not in_table?(slot):
        push_ready(step, id, w_revoked, slot, 0, 0, 0, 0)
        return unit
    gen = rget(slot, r_gen)
    if rget(slot, r_live) != 1:
        push_ready(step, id, w_revoked, slot, gen, 0, 0, 0)
        return unit
    kind = rget(slot, r_kind)
    if kind == kind_timer:
        now = ev.st.at(s_now)
        deadline = rget(slot, r_deadline)
        if deadline <= now:
            # Due already and nobody was waiting: the expiries since are
            # coalesced into this one wake.
            period = rget(slot, r_period)
            if period > 0:
                due = periods_due(deadline, period, now)
                rset(slot, r_deadline, deadline + due * period)
                push_ready(step, id, w_timer, slot, gen, due - 1, 0, due)
            else:
                rset(slot, r_inflight, rget(slot, r_inflight) + 1)
                push_ready(step, id, w_timer, slot, gen, 0, 0, 1)
                spend_timer(slot)
            return unit
    elif rget(slot, r_npend) > 0:
        take_queued(step, id, slot, gen)
        return unit
    tset(id, t_pos, ev.parked.length())
    tset(id, t_next, -1)
    ev.parked.push(step)
    ev.parked_id.push(id)
    tail = rget(slot, r_wtail)
    if tail == -1:
        rset(slot, r_whead, id)
    else:
        tset(tail, t_next, id)
    rset(slot, r_wtail, id)
    rset(slot, r_nwait, rget(slot, r_nwait) + 1)
    if rget(slot, r_keep) == 1:
        bump(s_keepwait)
    if kind == kind_timer and rget(slot, r_hpos) < 0:
        heap_insert(slot)

# What a segment's request means: -1 ready again; a slot, park there.
private nomethod fn after_segment(step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}, id: int, code: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    if code == -1:
        push_ready(step, id, w_ready, -1, 0, 0, 0, 0)
    else:
        park(step, id, code)

# Task ID's STEP, done at its start: complete it (STEP is released where
# this function ends). A function of its own so that adopt_checked moves its
# STEP on every path: where a coroutine handle is released at the end of its
# lexical scope (a program whose coroutines may hold resources across a
# suspension: LEXICAL-RESOURCE-CLEANUP-EXPLORATION-MILESTONE-1.md), a handle
# moved on some paths and kept on others has no one release point.
private nomethod fn complete_at_start(step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}, id: int, first: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    if tracing_on?():
        note(cat("task ", tget(id, t_serial)).concat(cat(" done at start ", first)))
    complete(id, first)

private nomethod fn adopt_checked(step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}, first: int, context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    id = new_task()
    if std::coroutine::done?(step):
        complete_at_start(step, id, first)
    else:
        if tracing_on?():
            note(cat("adopt task ", tget(id, t_serial)).concat(cat(" wants ", first)))
        after_segment(step, id, first)
    tget(id, t_serial)

# Hand a task to the scheduler: STEP was constructed by the caller (its
# first segment already ran), FIRST is what that segment yielded (or
# returned). The handle moves into the Loop; the task's id is returned:
# ids are serial numbers, never reused.
nomethod fn adopt(step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}, first: int, context ev: Loop) -> int errors SchedulerFault:
    adopt_checked(step, first):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What tasks call (they yield to the scheduler)

# Run again in the next turn: continuous activity, which keeps the loop
# awake and polling.
fn yield_now():
    yield -1

private fn wake_of(code: int) -> Wake:
    if code == w_event:
        Wake::Event
    elif code == w_timer:
        Wake::Timer
    elif code == w_turn:
        Wake::Turn
    elif code == w_revoked:
        Wake::Revoked
    elif code == w_ready:
        Wake::Ready
    else:
        Wake::Empty

# Wait for REG's next event (or its timer, its turn, its revocation). A
# registration that is not live answers Wake::Revoked at once, without
# suspending.
#
# (wait and try_take build their Event where they return it, from the
# registers: the native backend then carries its fields to the caller
# without allocating -- an Event returned from a helper through a handler
# cost an allocation per event, bench/event-loop.tcl.)
fn wait(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> Event errors SchedulerFault:
    if not live?(reg):
        return Event {reason: Wake::Revoked, a: 0, b: 0, count: 0}
    yield reg.slot
    Event {reason: wake_of(reg_of(s_wake_reason)), a: reg_of(s_wake_a), b: reg_of(s_wake_b), count: reg_of(s_wake_count)}

# Take REG's first queued event into the take registers: what try_take
# answers (a w_* code).
private fn try_take_checked(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    if not live_checked?(reg):
        return w_revoked
    slot = reg.slot
    npend = rget(slot, r_npend)
    if npend == 0 or rget(slot, r_kind) == kind_timer:
        return w_empty
    node = rget(slot, r_phead)
    a = ev.events.at(node * es)
    b = ev.events.at(node * es + 1)
    count = ev.events.at(node * es + 2)
    next = ev.events.at(node * es + 3)
    rset(slot, r_phead, next)
    if next == -1:
        rset(slot, r_ptail, -1)
    rset(slot, r_npend, npend - 1)
    ev.e_free.push(node)
    release_if_room(slot)
    set_reg(s_take_a, a)
    set_reg(s_take_b, b)
    set_reg(s_take_count, count)
    w_event

# REG's next queued event without suspending: Wake::Event, Wake::Empty when
# nothing is queued, Wake::Revoked when REG is not live. A task that drains
# a queue this way handles a whole batch in one dispatch.
fn try_take(reg: Reg, context ev: Loop) -> Event errors SchedulerFault:
    code = try_take_checked(reg):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault
    if code == w_event:
        Event {reason: Wake::Event, a: reg_of(s_take_a), b: reg_of(s_take_b), count: reg_of(s_take_count)}
    else:
        Event {reason: wake_of(code), a: 0, b: 0, count: 0}

# Sleep DELAY microseconds (a one-shot timer of its own): Wake::Timer, or
# Wake::Revoked when the loop shut down meanwhile.
fn sleep(delay: int, context ev: Loop) -> Wake errors SchedulerFault:
    e = wait(after(delay))
    e.reason

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The turn

private nomethod fn task_failed(id: int, ending: int, value: int, text: str, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    set_reg(s_dispatching, 0)
    bump(s_failed)
    if tracing_on?():
        note(cat("task ", tget(id, t_serial)).concat(" failed ").concat(text))
    record_outcome(tget(id, t_serial), ending, value)
    retire_task(id)

# Resume task ID (handle STEP), already out of every queue.
private nomethod fn run_task(step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: [TaskFailed, IndexNotFound, SchedulerFault, DriverFault]}, id: int, context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    reason = tget(id, t_reason)
    slot = tget(id, t_slot)
    gen = tget(id, t_gen)
    valid = if reason == w_event or reason == w_timer or reason == w_turn:
        rget(slot, r_gen) == gen
    else:
        true
    if not valid:
        bump(s_suppressed)
        if tracing_on?():
            note(cat("suppress wake of task ", tget(id, t_serial)).concat(cat(" from revoked ", slot)))
    effective = if valid:
        reason
    else:
        w_revoked
    # A spent one-shot timer is freed once every wake it made is dispatched.
    if valid and reason == w_timer and rget(slot, r_period) == 0:
        left = rget(slot, r_inflight) - 1
        rset(slot, r_inflight, left)
        if left == 0 and rget(slot, r_live) == 0:
            free_reg(slot)
    set_reg(s_wake_reason, effective)
    set_reg(s_wake_slot, slot)
    # A suppressed wake carries nothing of the event it was for.
    wa = if valid:
        tget(id, t_a)
    else:
        0
    wb = if valid:
        tget(id, t_b)
    else:
        0
    wc = if valid:
        tget(id, t_count)
    else:
        0
    set_reg(s_wake_a, wa)
    set_reg(s_wake_b, wb)
    set_reg(s_wake_count, wc)
    set_reg(s_current, tget(id, t_serial))
    set_reg(s_dispatching, 1)
    bump(s_dispatched)
    set_reg(s_turn_dispatched, ev.st.at(s_turn_dispatched) + 1)
    if tracing_on?():
        note(cat("run task ", tget(id, t_serial)).concat(cat(" reason ", effective)))
    code = step():
        on TaskFailed {code}:
            task_failed(id, 1, code, cat("TaskFailed ", code))
            return unit
        on IndexNotFound:
            task_failed(id, 2, 0, "IndexNotFound")
            return unit
        on SchedulerFault:
            task_failed(id, 3, 0, "SchedulerFault")
            set_reg(s_faulted, 1)
            return unit
        on DriverFault {code}:
            task_failed(id, 4, code, cat("DriverFault ", code))
            return unit
    set_reg(s_dispatching, 0)
    if std::coroutine::done?(step):
        if tracing_on?():
            note(cat("task ", tget(id, t_serial)).concat(cat(" done ", code)))
        complete(id, code)
        return unit
    after_segment(step, id, code)

# What the scheduler does with a record the driver hands over: 1 delivered
# (or dropped by policy), 0 stale or invalid, 2 the driver must hold it.
private nomethod fn accept_record(slot: int, gen: int, a: int, b: int, context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    code = deliver(slot, gen, a, b)
    if code == dv_full:
        rset(slot, r_held, 1)
        bump(s_held)
        if tracing_on?():
            note(cat("hold record for ", slot))
        return 2
    if code == dv_stale or code == dv_invalid:
        0
    else:
        1

# Collect at most the budget of native records from the driver; returns how
# many were delivered.
private fn collect_records(context ev: Loop, d: Driver) -> int errors IndexNotFound, DriverFault:
    budget = ev.st.at(s_budget)
    set_reg(s_tmp_count, 0)
    set_reg(s_tmp_taken, 0)
    loop:
        if ev.st.at(s_tmp_taken) >= budget:
            break
        slot = d.take_record()
        if slot < 0:
            break
        bump(s_tmp_taken)
        r = accept_record(slot, d.record_gen(), d.record_a(), d.record_b())
        if r == 2:
            d.hold_record()
        else:
            set_reg(s_tmp_count, ev.st.at(s_tmp_count) + r)
    ev.st.at(s_tmp_count)

# Tell the driver which held sources may deliver again.
private fn release_holds(context ev: Loop, d: Driver) errors IndexNotFound, DriverFault:
    loop:
        if ev.releases.empty?():
            break
        slot = ev.releases.pop()
        bump(s_released)
        if tracing_on?():
            note(cat("release held records for ", slot))
        d.release_held(slot)

# Drop the entries of revoked observers from the observer list, in place
# (registration order kept; nothing allocated).
private fn compact_observers(context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    n = ev.observers.length()
    set_reg(s_tmp_i, 0)
    loop i from 0 to n:
        slot = ev.observers.at(i)
        gen = ev.observer_gens.at(i)
        if rget(slot, r_gen) == gen and rget(slot, r_live) == 1:
            k = ev.st.at(s_tmp_i)
            ev.observers.swap(k, slot)
            ev.observer_gens.swap(k, gen)
            set_reg(s_tmp_i, k + 1)
    loop:
        if ev.observers.length() <= ev.st.at(s_tmp_i):
            break
        ev.observers.pop()
        ev.observer_gens.pop()

# The observer phase: every turn registration live when it starts, in
# registration order, runs its first waiting task once.
private fn observe(context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    n = ev.observers.length()
    set_reg(s_tmp_obs, 0)
    set_reg(s_tmp_dead, 0)
    loop:
        i = ev.st.at(s_tmp_obs)
        if i >= n:
            break
        set_reg(s_tmp_obs, i + 1)
        slot = ev.observers.at(i)
        gen = ev.observer_gens.at(i)
        if rget(slot, r_gen) == gen and rget(slot, r_live) == 1:
            id = pop_waiter(slot)
            if id != -1:
                step = unpark(id)
                tset(id, t_reason, w_turn)
                tset(id, t_slot, slot)
                tset(id, t_gen, gen)
                tset(id, t_a, ev.st.at(s_turn))
                tset(id, t_b, 0)
                tset(id, t_count, 1)
                bump(s_observed)
                run_task(step, id)
        else:
            bump(s_tmp_dead)
    # Registrations made during the phase were appended after the snapshot
    # and stay; revoked ones leave the list.
    if ev.st.at(s_tmp_dead) > 0:
        compact_observers()

private fn turn_checked(context ev: Loop, d: Driver) -> int errors IndexNotFound, DriverFault, LoopReentered:
    if ev.st.at(s_dispatching) == 1:
        bump(s_reentered)
        fail LoopReentered
    bump(s_turn)
    set_reg(s_now, d.clock())
    set_reg(s_turn_dispatched, 0)
    release_holds()
    timers = expire_timers(ev.st.at(s_now))
    records = collect_records()
    collected = timers + records
    set_reg(s_collected, ev.st.at(s_collected) + records)
    n = ready_count()
    if tracing_on?():
        note(cat("collect timers ", timers).concat(cat(" records ", records)).concat(cat(" ready ", n)))
    set_reg(s_tmp_i, 0)
    loop:
        if ev.st.at(s_tmp_i) >= n:
            break
        bump(s_tmp_i)
        id = front_ready()
        run_task(pop_ready(), id)
    if collected + n > 0:
        observe()
    else:
        bump(s_idle_turns)
    n

# One scheduling turn without waiting (the driver has waited, or a host
# advances the loop): collect, dispatch, observe. Returns how many tasks
# its dispatch phase ran.
fn turn(context ev: Loop, d: Driver) -> int errors SchedulerFault, DriverFault, LoopReentered:
    n = turn_checked():
        on IndexNotFound:
            -1
    # -1: an internal index failed; s_faulted: a task received SchedulerFault.
    if n < 0 or reg_of(s_faulted) == 1:
        fail SchedulerFault
    n

# Whether anything can still happen: ready work, or a task waiting on a
# registration that keeps the loop alive.
private fn live_work?(context ev: Loop) -> bool errors IndexNotFound:
    ready_count() > 0 or ev.st.at(s_keepwait) > 0

private fn sync_checked(context ev: Loop, d: Driver) -> int errors IndexNotFound, DriverFault:
    set_reg(s_now, d.clock())
    ev.st.at(s_now)

# Read the driver's clock into the loop's time: the time after, every and
# now use until the next turn starts. The application calls it before
# registering timers outside the loop; tasks never need it.
fn sync(context ev: Loop, d: Driver) -> int errors SchedulerFault, DriverFault:
    sync_checked():
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

private fn stop_checked(context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    set_reg(s_stopping, 1)

# Ask run to return after the current turn. A Loop operation, so a task can
# make it (a quit command): it ends the current run only, and the
# application decides what follows (run again, shutdown).
fn stop(context ev: Loop) errors SchedulerFault:
    stop_checked():
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

private fn run_checked(context ev: Loop, d: Driver) -> int errors IndexNotFound, DriverFault, LoopReentered:
    if ev.st.at(s_dispatching) == 1:
        bump(s_reentered)
        fail LoopReentered
    set_reg(s_stopping, 0)
    set_reg(s_now, d.clock())
    first = ev.st.at(s_turn)
    loop:
        if ev.st.at(s_stopping) == 1:
            if tracing_on?():
                note("stopped")
            break
        if ev.st.at(s_faulted) == 1:
            break
        if not live_work?():
            if tracing_on?():
                note("no live work")
            break
        # Ready work: poll (0 is never after now); else the earliest
        # deadline a task waits on (-1: none).
        deadline = if ready_count() > 0:
            0
        else:
            next_deadline()
        available = d.block(deadline)
        if available < 0 and ready_count() == 0 and next_deadline() == -1:
            if tracing_on?():
                note("driver exhausted")
            break
        turn_checked()
    ev.st.at(s_turn) - first

# Drive the loop until nothing can happen any more (or stop, or a driver
# that can never deliver again). Returns the number of turns.
fn run(context ev: Loop, d: Driver) -> int errors SchedulerFault, DriverFault, LoopReentered:
    n = run_checked():
        on IndexNotFound:
            -1
    # -1: an internal index failed; s_faulted: a task received SchedulerFault.
    if n < 0 or reg_of(s_faulted) == 1:
        fail SchedulerFault
    n

private fn abandon_checked(context ev: Loop) -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    dropped = ready_count() + ev.parked.length()
    # Every waiter is one of the tasks dropped below: no registration has one
    # left -- first, so that releasing a task's registrations as it is dropped
    # (its frames' Owners, LEXICAL-RESOURCE-CLEANUP-EXPLORATION-MILESTONE-1.md)
    # wakes nobody and moves no task between the queues being drained.
    loop slot from 0 to ev.st.at(s_nregs):
        rset(slot, r_whead, -1)
        rset(slot, r_wtail, -1)
        rset(slot, r_nwait, 0)
        rset(slot, r_hpos, -1)
    ev.heap.clear()
    set_reg(s_keepwait, 0)
    loop:
        if ev.ready_in.empty?():
            break
        retire_task(ev.ready_in_id.pop())
        ev.ready_in.pop()
    loop:
        if ev.ready_out.empty?():
            break
        retire_task(ev.ready_out_id.pop())
        ev.ready_out.pop()
    loop:
        if ev.parked.empty?():
            break
        retire_task(ev.parked_id.pop())
        ev.parked.pop()
    if tracing_on?():
        note(cat("abandon ", dropped))
    dropped

# Release every task the loop still owns, without running it (a released
# coroutine is unwound, never resumed): the end of a shutdown. Returns how
# many it released. The application's, as run and shutdown are (it
# requires the Driver: a library holding only the Loop cannot drop the
# program's tasks); from inside a dispatch it is LoopReentered.
fn abandon(context ev: Loop, d: Driver) -> int errors SchedulerFault, LoopReentered:
    if reg_of(s_dispatching) == 1:
        fail LoopReentered
    abandon_checked():
        on IndexNotFound:
            fail SchedulerFault

private fn revoke_all(context ev: Loop) errors IndexNotFound:
    loop slot from 0 to ev.st.at(s_nregs):
        if rget(slot, r_kind) != kind_free:
            revoke_slot(Reg {slot: slot, gen: rget(slot, r_gen)})

private fn shutdown_checked(rounds: int, context ev: Loop, d: Driver) -> int errors IndexNotFound, DriverFault, LoopReentered:
    if ev.st.at(s_dispatching) == 1:
        bump(s_reentered)
        fail LoopReentered
    if tracing_on?():
        note("shutdown")
    revoke_all()
    loop r from 0 to rounds:
        if ready_count() == 0:
            break
        turn_checked()
    abandon_checked()

# Shutdown: revoke every registration (their waiters wake with
# Wake::Revoked and may finish), run at most ROUNDS more turns while tasks
# are ready, then abandon what is left. Returns how many tasks were
# abandoned.
fn shutdown(rounds: int, context ev: Loop, d: Driver) -> int errors SchedulerFault, DriverFault, LoopReentered:
    n = shutdown_checked(rounds):
        on IndexNotFound:
            -1
    # -1: an internal index failed; s_faulted: a task received SchedulerFault.
    if n < 0 or reg_of(s_faulted) == 1:
        fail SchedulerFault
    n
